WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.070
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.710
I spent years trying to see
All That Jazz on the big screen.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.437
I see a lot of movies, and
it's one of my absolute favorites.

00:00:15.051 --> 00:00:18.150
It's kind of a musical,
and it's definitely sexy.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.100
It reckons with Bob Fosse,
the Cabaret in Chicago Guy, as he chases

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.850
his own ambitions rather than cultivating
relationships with the people he loves.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.310
Because of that,
it's a movie that all of us should see.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.520
I had the chance to see All That Jazz on
a pristine 35 millimeter film print this

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.420
summer in Los Angeles.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.960
The energy of the crowd was infectious,

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.020
as we all took in this
beautiful film together.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.263
Even my girlfriend, who hates musicals
because they're not cynical enough,

00:00:49.263 --> 00:00:51.124
walked away, impressed by the film.

00:00:53.806 --> 00:00:56.720
I made her have the chance to see
All That Jazz in a theatre again.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.020
It's a Fox movie.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.820
And after Disney acquired Fox,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.350
they've slowly started pulling all Fox
films from theatrical distribution.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.330
Copyright lasts 95 years, so

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.880
this movie might not be in
a theater again until 2075.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:20.410
Now, you might not care about All That
Jazz, but what about Alien, or Titanic?

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:21.920
These are classic Fox films,

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.496
and they might not be in theatres
again until after most of us are gone.

00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:31.810
Ideas are too important for
someone to own forever.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.780
The public domain allows all of us to
own creative ideas like stories and

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:38.480
characters.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.560
The problem is copyright
keeps getting longer,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.200
and this is preventing new work
from entering the public domain.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.790
Imagine a world where the idea for

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:57.080
the light bulb and its production has been
owned by one person since it was invented.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.070
The world would literally
be a darker place.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.030
The light bulbs would
be insanely expensive,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.710
and there probably wouldn't be much
innovation on the light bulb front.

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.220
Now think about a world where you
had to pay Shakespeare's estate

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.840
every time you adapted one of his plays.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.440
Amazing movies like Ten Things I
Hate About You, Westside Story, or

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.430
The Lion King, might not exist if
studios had to pay for these rights.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.400
Thankfully, these plays
are in the public domain.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.760
So creators are able to pull from them to
create any crazy thing they can imagine.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.534
This has the added benefit of keeping
his stories and characters alive.

00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:43.650
Plays and stories written in the 20th
century are not as lucky as Shakespeare's.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.559
This is because copyright
keeps getting longer.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.610
At the start of the 20th century,
copyright lasted 42 years.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.900
This eventually bumped up to 56 years,
75 years, and

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.520
95 years, their lobbying efforts.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.100
The result of this is that no new work
enter the public domain from 1998 to 2019.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.331
That's 20 years of stories sitting
on shelves when they could be

00:03:09.331 --> 00:03:11.073
thriving before our eyes.

00:03:14.002 --> 00:03:18.610
We've decided that some ideas are not too
important for someone to own forever.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.638
Copyright last 95 years,
but patents only last 20.

00:03:24.638 --> 00:03:29.973
That's tacit acknowledgment that as
a society we think that transistors and

00:03:29.973 --> 00:03:33.760
telephones matter less than our books and
our movies.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.790
I strongly disagree with this.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.030
Technology can lengthen or
improve our lives, but

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.299
to quote author Joan Didion, we tell
ourselves stories in order to live.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.017
Only through stories can you step outside
your life and into somebody else's life.

00:03:55.357 --> 00:03:59.200
The public domain actually
makes work more valuable.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.100
Let's take a look at the Wizard of Oz.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:08.440
The original novel was published
by L Frank Baum, in 1900.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.630
MGM purchased the rights, and

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.560
in 1939 they released that
famous Judy Garland adaptation.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:19.460
The thing is,
copyright was shorter in the 20th century.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:23.600
So by the 1970s, most of the novels and

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.250
the sequels of the novels
entered the public domain.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.595
This opened up the floodgates for
new adaptations of the Wizard of Oz.

00:04:30.595 --> 00:04:34.930
Adaptations like sci-fi channel's Tin Man,
or

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:39.195
Disney's really bizarre movie,
Return to Oz.

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:42.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; These are kind of creepy examples,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.530
so they're probably
working against my point.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.060
But what about something unimpeachable
like The Wiz, or Wicked?

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.766
I love these musicals, and you might too.

00:04:52.766 --> 00:04:56.910
And they might not exist
without the public domain.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.240
The public domain has created
far more economic value out

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.181
of the Wizard of Oz than just royalty
payments to L Frank Baum and his heirs.

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:09.270
It's also kept his stories alive.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.300
Mickey Mouse represents a world of
more restrictive copyright terms.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:18.990
Mickey is ubiquitous.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.240
You can find them on iPhone cases,
or dish towels.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.420
You might even have photos of
him from your family vacation.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.660
But that's the problem,
Mickey's no longer a character.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.650
He's a piece of corporate iconography.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.590
What was the last great
Mickey Mouse cartoon?

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:38.526
I would argue, Fantasia?

00:05:38.526 --> 00:05:40.967
And that came out in 1940.

00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:45.970
Disney is not telling great
Mickey Mouse stories.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:50.294
And Mickey's soul has been trapped
in the Disney vault since 1928.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.090
There's good news though.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:59.345
Mickey's going to be free in just four
years, his copyright expires in 2024.

00:05:59.345 --> 00:06:02.460
Let me tell you what's going to happen.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.760
I promise you,
Disney is going to be just fine.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:10.470
First of all, the copyright only covers
the original Mickey Mouse cartoons like

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.370
Steamboat Willie Any
updates to his character or

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.460
his design are covered
under future copyrights.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.610
So they're still covered.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.980
Any of the imagery that is
used in corporate logos

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.590
actually counts as trademark and
that lasts forever.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.980
Disney's done an amazing job building
an intellectual property moat

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.630
with characters like Goofy,
or Donald Duck.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:39.070
These characters came out later, so
the copyrights are going to expire later.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:44.590
All Mickey expiring is going to do is
incentivize Disney to tell the best

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:47.290
Mickey Mouse stories they can,
on a more open market.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:53.075
Now, I'm not advocating for
an abolition of copyright.

00:06:53.075 --> 00:06:57.559
I want to make movies one day, and
I think creators deserve to be paid for

00:06:57.559 --> 00:06:58.400
their work.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.315
Copyright protects that right.

00:07:01.315 --> 00:07:05.052
I also recognize that it's
probably impractical to shorten

00:07:05.052 --> 00:07:07.771
copyright terms from where they stand now.

00:07:07.771 --> 00:07:10.720
I just want to make sure
they don't get any longer.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.360
Characters like Mickey deserve to be free.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:20.290
Remember, ideas are too important for
someone to own forever.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.510
Remember this, if you ever have the chance
to work with content in your career,

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:30.839
your instinct may be to hoard this content
by advocating for longer copy right terms.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:33.147
Please don't do this.

00:07:33.147 --> 00:07:34.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.084
&gt;&gt; After 95 years, stories and characters
are better off in the hands of everyone.

00:07:42.084 --> 00:07:44.058
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:07:44.058 --> 00:07:48.601
[MUSIC]

